                'content' => "Chemical control is only one tool, and often the last one a wise farmer reaches for. Biological control and beneficials is the discipline of using nature's own controls to keep pests below damaging levels.\n\nStart by knowing the beneficials. Many insects, mites, and birds eat the pests that attack coconut palms, and a healthy farm supports them. The farmer who can recognise the beneficial, and does not kill it with a broad spray, has a free workforce in the field.\n\nProtect and encourage the beneficials. Avoid unnecessary sprays, especially broad-spectrum ones that kill the pest's enemies along with the pest. Provide habitat, and the beneficial population builds. The farm that shelters its beneficials needs fewer sprays over the years.\n\nIntroduce where nature needs help. In some systems, specific natural enemies can be released against a specific pest. Done with knowledge, and after checking the pest is present and the release conditions are right, it is a powerful, targeted tool. Done blindly, it wastes money and time.\n\nFinally, be patient. Biological control is slower than a spray, and it aims at balance, not eradication. The farmer who uses beneficials, and gives them time to work, builds a farm that controls itself, season after season, with less chemical and less cost.",
