                'title' => 'Weather, Climate and Pest Cycles',
                'summary' => 'Time the control to the season.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Pests and diseases do not appear at random; they follow the weather and the seasons, and the farmer who understands the cycles controls them earlier and cheaper. Weather, climate and pest cycles is the discipline of timing the work.\n\nStart with the season. Many pests flare with the rains, and others peak in the dry, hot months. The farm records of past seasons show the pattern, and the farmer who knows the pattern expects the outbreak rather than being surprised by it.\n\nWatch the weather. Rain, humidity, and temperature drive pest and disease development, and a warm, wet spell is a warning to scout. The farmer who watches the forecast and the signs is the farmer who acts before the pest spreads, while the control is still easy and cheap.\n\nUse the climate wisely. The changing climate shifts the seasons and the pests, and the farmer who notices a new pest, or an old pest in a new season, adapts the plan. The farm that tracks the change adjusts its calendar rather than repeating a plan that no longer fits.\n\nFinally, plan the control around the cycle. A treatment timed to the pest's vulnerable stage, before the damage, is a treatment that works; one timed after the damage is a treatment that only records the loss. Timing is the cheapest control there is.",
