                "content" => "The cheapest pest control is the pest that never arrives. Quarantine and biosecurity is the discipline of keeping new pests, diseases, and weeds off the farm, because an introduced pest has no natural enemies and no history of control on the farm.\n\nStart at the gate. What comes onto the farm, on tools, tyres, boots, animals, and new planting material, can carry a pest or a disease. The farm that cleans equipment, controls visitor movement, and inspects incoming material closes the gate that most pests walk through.\n\nUse clean planting material. New seedlings and budwood should come from a certified, clean source, because an infected seedling is an infected palm for its whole life. The farmer who buys certified material buys decades of freedom from the diseases the cheap seedling carries.\n\nIsolate before you plant. A new seedling or an incoming plant should be observed in isolation before it joins the farm. The farmer who isolates catches the introduced disease in the first weeks, when it is contained, rather than in the first season, when it is spread.\n\nFinally, report the unknown. A pest or symptom you cannot identify is a risk to the whole region, and reporting it early protects everyone. The farmer who guards the gate, and reports the unknown, protects their farm and their neighbours' farms too.",
