                'content' => "Pests do not respect farm boundaries, and the pest on the neighbour's farm is the pest on your farm next season. Community pest management is the discipline of managing pests together, across farms, because a pest managed alone is a pest managed halfway.\n\nStart by coordinating the calendar. If neighbouring farms plant, harvest, and spray on the same calendar, the pest has no refuge and no continuous host. The community that aligns its planting and its control breaks the pest's cycle across the whole area, not just one farm.\n\nShare the monitoring. Many eyes spot an outbreak earlier than one. The community that scouts together, and reports the first sighting, catches the pest while it is local and before it spreads to every farm in the area.\n\nAgree the approach. A community that agrees on the pest to control, the timing, and the method, and avoids the drift that poisons a neighbour's field, controls better and safer. The farmer who sprays alone can spray a neighbour's crop by mistake; the community that coordinates controls precisely.\n\nFinally, build the habit. A community pest meeting, a shared calendar, and a shared record turn scattered farms into a managed landscape. The community that manages pests together harvests together, and the pest that has no refuge across the landscape is the pest that is finally beaten.",
