                'summary' => 'A cooperative lives on its members.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A cooperative is its members, and a cooperative whose members do not participate is a cooperative that slowly fails. Member engagement and participation is the discipline of keeping members active, informed, and invested in the shared work.\n\nStart with communication. Members who know what the cooperative does, what it has achieved, and what it plans, participate; members who hear nothing drift away. Regular, clear communication to every member, in the language they use, is the foundation of engagement.\n\nGive the members a real voice. Meetings where decisions are explained and members can ask, question, and vote are how a cooperative stays a cooperative rather than a society run by a few. The leader who listens at the meeting keeps the members' trust, and trust is the cooperative's capital.\n\nShare the benefits visibly. When members see what the cooperative has won for them, a better price, a shared service, a market, they re-engage. The cooperative that distributes its benefits fairly and visibly earns the participation that keeps it alive.\n\nFinally, welcome and train the new members. Every new member is a new participant or a new dropout, depending on how they are received. The cooperative that onboards, trains, and involves its new members grows stronger with each member, and participation is the cooperative's heartbeat.",
