                "content" => "A cooperative holds its members' money, produce, and trust, and risk management and internal control is the discipline of guarding all three. The failures that end cooperatives are usually avoidable, and the controls that prevent them are simple.\n\nStart with the separation of duties. The person who handles the money should not be the only person who records it, approves it, and checks it. When duties are separated, an error or a theft needs two people to hide it, and the cooperative that separates its duties protects its members' funds.\n\nKeep the records and the audit trail. Every transaction, from member contribution to purchase to sale, should be recorded and traceable to a source document. The cooperative whose books any member can follow is the cooperative whose members trust it, and trust is the hardest thing to rebuild.\n\nControl the physical assets and the stock. Store, count, and reconcile the cooperative's produce and assets, and investigate any gap. The cooperative that reconciles its stock catches the loss while it is small.\n\nFinally, manage the known risks: a failed crop, a defaulting buyer, a price crash. Each needs a plan and, where possible, a reserve. The cooperative that controls its internal risks and plans for the external ones is the cooperative that endures, and endurance is the members' security.",
