                'content' => "A cooperative is led by its board, and the quality of that leadership decides the cooperative's future. Leadership and board development is the discipline of growing leaders who serve the members, not themselves.\n\nStart with the board's role. The board governs: it sets the direction, guards the members' interests, and hires and oversees the management. A board that does the manager's work, or that abdicates it, is a board that fails the members. The board that stays in its role keeps the cooperative on course.\n\nBuild the skills. A board needs to read a budget, ask the hard questions, and plan strategically, and those skills are learned. The cooperative that trains its board invests in every decision the board will make, and a trained board is a cooperative's greatest asset.\n\nKeep the board accountable to the members. Elected, reported to, and replaceable by the members, the board serves at the members' pleasure. The cooperative whose board reports honestly and welcomes the members' questions is the cooperative whose leaders serve rather than rule.\n\nFinally, plan the succession. A board that recruits, mentors, and rotates its members is a board that renews itself. The cooperative that develops its leaders, and hands over the leadership with a plan, is the cooperative that outlasts any one leader, and that is the members' real security.",
